leads.fragile_hypothesis:
The fragile hypothesis was: "a basis designed for hard saturation, placed after a prefilter, brings G5 below 0.6", i.e. basis shape is the bottleneck. Its weak points:
- it relies on one hand-picked prefilter (300 Hz, chosen by an out-of-protocol scratch scan on take 20);
- it uses the instantaneous |z| in place of an RF envelope;
- the knots are fixed multiples of the RMS on A.
The measurements isolate shape from prefilter: saturating vs polynomial matters a lot, but which saturating shape is used does not.

Given hypothesis: a DI is not a multisine, and the BLA under Gaussian excitation depends on the input distribution. It is confirmed, and that refutes using the multisine as a DI ceiling.

Fragile point of my proposed rule: its margin is the difference of one paired quantity between the two 15 s halves of V (n = 2). It is a crude estimate of the spread. Several cells sit within 0.02 of their margin and are undecidable:
- G2.5 H5: -30 (0.098 vs 0.086), -24 (0.097 vs 0.091);
- G5 H5: -24 (0.082 vs 0.085), -18 (0.031 vs 0.026);
- G7.5 H5: -24 (0.084 vs 0.088);
- G10 H5: -24 (0.145 vs 0.125);
- G2.5 VL3: -18 (0.061 vs 0.083).

The rule's reference is the frozen FIR (A fit), which is itself a probe and depends on its frozen ridge of 1e-2.

The absolute linear ceiling is content-dependent. The level-scheduled FIR leaves a share that differs by a median 29 % (max 36 %) between the two halves of V. This is why the proposed rule is paired and relative, not absolute.

