leads.verdict_reason:
Two separate conclusions.

The hypothesis that a state or feedback beyond the probe memory explains the H2 refusal is refuted.
- Reaching 0.3 needs a mechanism carrying at least 0.45-0.50 of the output RMS at G >= 2.5.
- The history-dependent share measured model-free is at most 0.041 beyond 30 ms and at most 0.0094 beyond 100 ms (multisine). Step envelopes move by at most 0.24 dB beyond 30 ms.
- Even the worst case, the click onsets (-20.4 dB, about 0.1), would lower capacity by at most 0.1.
- The direct measurements on DI agree:
  - causal LPV at tau >= 100 ms gains 0.012-0.021;
  - the output-level oracle gains 0.008-0.027 at G >= 2.5 and loses at G0;
  - ARX free-run gains are negative (-0.005 to -0.0003);
  - the polynomial NARX diverges;
  - the history contrast is at most 0.058.

The pre-registered LPV rule itself returned "inconclusive" (drops 0.036-0.043 around the 0.04 threshold). I report it unchanged. The refutation rests on the triangle bound and the model-free measurements, not on that rule. The 10 ms LPV gain is explained by the controls: the anticausal envelope does as well or better, the placebo never helps.

The existence of a slow state is established, separately:
- click onsets that are not relaxed at 0.5 s and are relaxed by 1 s;
- multisine relaxation over 30-100 ms, about 10 dB above the floor;
- regrowth in silence after loud input at presence 10, G >= 5.
